# Navigation and deep-link security

Deep links, universal links, and push-notification payloads are **untrusted input** from
outside the app boundary. Any app or webpage on the device can fire them with arbitrary
parameters.

## Link scheme

- Prefer **verified links** — iOS Universal Links and Android App Links with
  server-hosted association files — over custom URL schemes; custom schemes can be
  claimed by other apps.
- Keep a custom scheme only as a fallback, and never for authentication callbacks unless
  using PKCE (the code is useless without the verifier).

## Deep-link validation (mandatory)

- One central linking configuration maps URLs to routes — no ad-hoc URL parsing in screens.
- Every deep-link-reachable route validates its params before rendering:
  - Type-narrow every param (`unknown` → validated type); reject or fall back on failure.
  - IDs are validated for format; the target entity is fetched with the user's own
    authorization — the link never grants access by itself.
  - Reject unexpected params silently rather than passing them through.
- Malformed links route to a safe default (home) — never a crash, never a blank screen,
  never an authenticated action.

## Authenticated routing

- Links targeting authenticated screens are **queued, not executed** while logged out: the
  user authenticates first, then the pending link is validated and dispatched.
- Sensitive actions (payments, deletions, credential changes) are never directly
  executable from a link — the link may open the screen; the user confirms the action.

## Outbound navigation

- `Linking.openURL` targets are allow-listed by scheme (`https`, `mailto`, `tel`) —
  never open a URL built from unvalidated external input.
- External URLs from API content open in the system browser or a browser view — with the
  URL visible to the user, not silently in-app.

## WebView hardening (when a WebView is unavoidable)

- Allow-list the origins the WebView may load (`originWhitelist`); block navigation
  elsewhere via `onShouldStartLoadWithRequest`.
- `javaScriptEnabled` only when required; never enable file access
  (`allowFileAccess={false}`, no `file://` URLs).
- Never inject secrets or tokens into WebView JS contexts; communicate through scoped
  `postMessage` handlers that validate every message.
- Never render authentication forms for your own service in a WebView — use system
  browser sessions (see `security/04-authentication.mdc`).

## Push payloads

- Notification payloads follow the same rule as deep links: validate the routing data,
  authorize server-side, and treat display strings as untrusted content.
